        "type": "reg",
        "input": "12\n1\n0\n1\n1\n1\n10\n2\n0 1\n2\n1 0\n2\n3 2\n2\n3 3\n2\n3 4\n2\n10 43\n5\n2 1 1 4 3\n5\n1 2 2 2 2\n5\n8 6 5 1 8",
        "output": "no\nyes\nyes\nyes\nyes\nno\nno\nyes\nyes\nno\nyes\nyes",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nExplanation of the test cases 1, 2, 3:\nFor $N=1$, one can prove that $\\mu((0)) = 1$ and $\\mu((k))=0$ for all $k\\ge 1$. \n\n \n It holds $\\mu((0)) = 1\\not=0$, so the answer to testcase 1 is no. \n It holds $\\mu((1)) = 0$, so the answer to testcase 2 is yes. \n It holds $\\mu((10)) = 0$, so the answer to testcase 3 is yes. \n\n\nExplanation of testcases 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9:\nFor $N=2$, one can prove that $\\mu((0,0))=1$, $\\mu((k, k)) = 1$ and $\\mu((k, k-1))=-1$ for all $k\\ge 2$, and all other values of $\\mu$ are $0$.\nLet us check that the defining property of $\\mu$ holds for $x=(2, 2)$ (notice that $x$ is special in this case):\n$$ 1 = \\sum_{y\\le x} \\mu(y) \n= \\mu(0, 0) + \\mu(0, 1) + \\mu(0, 2) + \n\\mu(1, 0) + \\mu(1, 1) + \\mu(1, 2)  + \n\\mu(2, 0) + \\mu(2, 1) + \\mu(2, 2) $$\n$$ = 1 + 0 + 0 + 0 + 0 + 0 + 0 - 1 + 1 = 1 .\\hspace{13em}$$\n\n\n It holds $\\mu((0, 1)) = 0$, so the answer to testcase 4 is yes. \n It holds $\\mu((1, 0)) = 0$, so the answer to testcase 5 is yes. \n It holds $\\mu((3, 2)) = -1$, so the answer to testcase 6 is no. \n It holds $\\mu((3, 3)) = 1$, so the answer to testcase 7 is no. \n It holds $\\mu((3, 4)) = 0$, so the answer to testcase 8 is yes. \n It holds $\\mu((10, 43)) = 0$, so the answer to testcase 9 is yes."
      }
